=== REASONING ===

Why this design preserves ADR-015 + the Phase-50 methodology:

P1 (hexagonal port-adapter): the new internal/ai/provider package
defines exactly one port — provider.Provider — and four adapters
(ollama, openai, anthropic, mock) that implement it. Feature code
(F4 dispatch, F5 stream, F7 embed) imports only the port; adapter
selection happens through the registry, never via direct import.
Adding a fifth provider is a one-package addition with no edits to
any feature code.

P5 (cross-cutting decorators): provider.Decorator + Chain define the
seam where future audit (F3), redaction (F8), rate/cost cap (F9)
plug in. Order is documented (Trace -> Audit -> CostCap -> RateLimit
-> Redaction -> base). F1 ships only WithTrace; the chain is
populated in later slices without re-touching the base adapters or
the registry contract.

D2 / D3 (mode-based selection + per-feature override):
ResolveProviderName implements the documented precedence —
feature override beats explicit "default" key beats DefaultProviderForMode.
This keeps the 14-feature roadmap (each picks its own provider via
the override map without changing top-level mode).

D14 (R3 mitigation — DNS rebinding): ValidateLocal is the gate
applied at config-save time (F2 will call it). It validates the
URL parses, the scheme is http(s), the host resolves to ALL-private
IPs, and pins the resolved IP into ProviderConfig.PinnedIP. The
runtime helper CheckPinnedIP re-resolves on every request and rejects
when ANY current resolution is non-private OR the pin no longer
appears in DNS.

ADR-015 invariants explicitly preserved:

I1 (default-off): the registry returns ErrProviderDisabled when
mode=off (defence in depth — the guard 404s upstream). Verified by
TestRegistry_For_OffMode_ReturnsErrProviderDisabled.

I3 (baseline intact): F1 is a foundation slice. No baseline handler,
worker, page, or hook is replaced. The new health endpoint has no
"non-AI baseline" because it does not exist in the off path.

I4 (zero egress in off mode): adapters never construct HTTP clients
at boot; only inside Chat / Stream / Embed. Off-mode short-circuits
in the registry before any adapter instantiation. The Builder is
called only after the mode + feature gate has passed.

I5 (hidden UI): the new ai-provider-health feature has empty
Frontend / UITestIDs. ops-only diagnostic; no SPA surface.

I6 (404 routes): the new GET /api/v1/ai/_internal/health is mounted
through guard.Wrap("ai-provider-health", ...) AND the existing
TestGuard_OffModeReturns404 walks features.IDs() and asserts 404 for
every Backend route — including the new entry. PASS captured below.

I7 (per-feature opt-in, default off): ai-provider-health entry has
DefaultOn=false. CoverageOK() rejects DefaultOn=true at compile time.

I9 (provider keys never leak in off mode): handler returns 404 in
off mode; HealthSnapshot returns ErrProviderDisabled in off mode
even if reached. Verified by TestRegistry_HealthSnapshot_OffMode.

I10 (type system): every /api/v1/ai/* route is mounted via guard.Wrap.
aivet PASS captured below — 2 AI route(s), 2 feature(s) in registry,
TS mirror in sync.

R5 mitigation (settings repo single-purpose): instead of adding an
AIProviderConfig method to *database.SettingsRepo, an inline
aiSettingsReader adapter type in router.go calls Get(ctx) and
returns the AIProviderConfig field. Keeps the settings repo's
public surface unchanged in this slice.

